🐛 Describe the bug
F.linear (and nn.Linear) with a bias on a 3D input silently returns wrong results on MPS when the leading batch dimension exceeds 65536 (2^16). The corruption is silent (no error/warning), affects a subset of batch rows, and appears nondeterministic across identical calls.
import torch
import torch.nn.functional as F

torch.manual_seed(0)
x = torch.randn(65537, 11, 128)
w = torch.randn(512, 128)
b = torch.randn(512)

ref = F.linear(x, w, b)
out = F.linear(x.to("mps"), w.to("mps"), b.to("mps")).cpu()

print((out - ref).abs().max())  # ~60 (!) — expected ~1e-5
print((~torch.isclose(out, ref, atol=1e-3, rtol=1e-3)).any(-1).any(-1).sum().item(), "corrupted rows")
Reproduces identically on 2.12.0, 2.13.0 (latest stable), and nightly 2.14.0.dev20260709.
Each of the following makes the result exactly correct:

B <= 65536 (the threshold is exactly 65536 → 65537)
bias=None (e.g. nn.Linear(..., bias=False))
reshaping the input to 2D first: F.linear(x.reshape(-1, 128), w, b)
fp16/bf16 input (on M5), or a non-contiguous input — see root cause for why

Root cause
The corruption is a 16-bit batch-index wraparound inside Apple's MPSNDArrayMatrixMultiplication fused 3-source (A@B+C) kernel: batch b is written to output slot b mod 65536. PyTorch's _mps_linear_nograph (aten/src/ATen/native/mps/operations/Linear.mm) hits it because the bias path encodes that kernel with sourceCount:3 on the unflattened 3D NDArray.
Evidence, all on Apple M5 Max / macOS 26.5.2:

Output forensics (through PyTorch). At B=65537, output row 0 equals row 65536's correct result to float precision (max diff 9.5e-6), i.e. batch 65536 wrapped onto slot 0.
Pure Objective-C repro, no PyTorch (attached below), mirroring Linear.mm's NDArray setup:
B=  65536 bias=1  bad rows:      0
B=  65537 bias=1  bad rows:     65  first bad: 0  row0==row65536's result: YES
B=  80000 bias=1  bad rows:  28928  first bad: 0  row0==row65536's result: YES
B=  80000 bias=0  bad rows:      0

The 28,928 count is exactly 2 × (80000 − 65536): each wrapped batch clobbers one victim row below 65536 and leaves its own output row unwritten. (The ObjC repro poisons the output buffer to show the unwritten rows; through PyTorch those rows expose recycled heap memory, which is why results look nondeterministic and allocator-state-dependent.) The corrupted-row count through PyTorch at B=80000 is also exactly 28,928.
The 2-source (no-bias) variant of the same kernel is correct at all sizes tested — hence bias=False being clean.
Path analysis explains every workaround: fp16/bf16 on M5 is clean because needs_nd_workaround() (added for #180776 — a different known defect in this same kernel family on M5) flattens those inputs to 2D before encoding; non-contiguous inputs are clean because they fall back to the MPSGraph path; 2D inputs are clean because the wraparound is in the NDArray batch dimension, not matrix rows (a 2D input with 721,907 rows is exact).

Related precedent: the MPS convolution kernel had a 2^16 output-channel limit, guarded with TORCH_CHECK_NOT_IMPLEMENTED and fixed by Apple in macOS 15.1 (#129207, #142515). The now-closed #122045 (macOS-13-only F.linear corruption) is a different bug on a code path that no longer exists.
Suggested fix
For a contiguous input with bias.dim() <= 1, input.flatten(0, -2) is a free view and sidesteps the broken kernel entirely — the machinery already exists in Linear.mm as the #180776 workaround. Extending that flatten from "fp16/bf16 on Apple10+" to also cover "leading-dims product > 65536" (any dtype) fixes this with no perf cost. Alternatively guard like Convolution.mm does, but the silent fix seems strictly better here. Note PYTORCH_MPS_PREFER_METAL does not reroute F.linear (#188728), so users currently have no opt-out knob. This should presumably also be reported to Apple via Feedback Assistant, since the defect is in the MPS framework kernel itself.
How this was found
Evaluating a small transformer on an 80k-example dataset in a single batch: train-set metrics looked mysteriously worse than test-set metrics (40k rows) because only the train eval crossed the 2^16 threshold — the model's FeedForward nn.Linear layers (bias=True) corrupted ~36% of rows.
Minimal pure-MPS reproducer (no PyTorch)

mps_ndarray_repro.m — creates fp32 MPSNDArrays input (B,T,K), weight (N,K) with transposeDimension:0:1 + preferPackedRows (exactly as Linear.mm does), bias (N); encodes MPSNDArrayMatrixMultiplication with sourceCount:3; validates against CPU arithmetic. B=65537 corrupts, B=65536 exact; sourceCount:2 exact at any B.
// Pure-MPS repro: MPSNDArrayMatrixMultiplication (3-source, A@B+C) writes batch
// index b to b mod 65536 when the leading dim of a 3D source exceeds 2^16.
// Mirrors PyTorch's _mps_linear_nograph setup (Linear.mm).
//
// Build: clang -fobjc-arc -framework Foundation -framework Metal \
//        -framework MetalPerformanceShaders mps_ndarray_repro.m -o mps_ndarray_repro
#import <Foundation/Foundation.h>
#import <Metal/Metal.h>
#import <MetalPerformanceShaders/MetalPerformanceShaders.h>

static const NSUInteger T = 2, K = 32, N = 32;

static float inputVal(NSUInteger b, NSUInteger t, NSUInteger k) {
  return (float)((b * 31 + t * 7 + k * 3) % 13) - 6.0f;
}
static float weightVal(NSUInteger n, NSUInteger k) {  // stored (N, K), used as K x N
  return (float)((n * 5 + k) % 7) - 3.0f;
}
static float biasVal(NSUInteger n) { return (float)(n % 5) - 2.0f; }

static float expectedOut(NSUInteger b, NSUInteger t, NSUInteger n) {
  float acc = biasVal(n);
  for (NSUInteger k = 0; k < K; k++) acc += inputVal(b, t, k) * weightVal(n, k);
  return acc;
}

static void runCase(id<MTLDevice> dev, id<MTLCommandQueue> queue, NSUInteger B, BOOL withBias) {
  id<MTLBuffer> inBuf = [dev newBufferWithLength:B * T * K * 4 options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared];
  id<MTLBuffer> wBuf = [dev newBufferWithLength:N * K * 4 options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared];
  id<MTLBuffer> bBuf = [dev newBufferWithLength:N * 4 options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared];
  id<MTLBuffer> outBuf = [dev newBufferWithLength:B * T * N * 4 options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared];

  float *in = inBuf.contents, *w = wBuf.contents, *bias = bBuf.contents, *out = outBuf.contents;
  for (NSUInteger b = 0; b < B; b++)
    for (NSUInteger t = 0; t < T; t++)
      for (NSUInteger k = 0; k < K; k++) in[(b * T + t) * K + k] = inputVal(b, t, k);
  for (NSUInteger n = 0; n < N; n++)
    for (NSUInteger k = 0; k < K; k++) w[n * K + k] = weightVal(n, k);
  for (NSUInteger n = 0; n < N; n++) bias[n] = biasVal(n);
  memset(out, 0xAA, B * T * N * 4);  // poison so untouched rows are detectable

  // shapes follow MPSNDArrayDescriptor convention (fastest-moving dim first),
  // matching PyTorch's getMPSShape(): input (B, T, K) -> @[K, T, B] reversed? No:
  // MPS wants sizes in the same order as PyTorch passes them via getMPSShape,
  // which lists them slowest-first. descriptorWithDataType:shape: takes an
  // NSArray in row-major (slowest-first) order.
  MPSNDArrayDescriptor *inDesc =
      [MPSNDArrayDescriptor descriptorWithDataType:MPSDataTypeFloat32
                                             shape:@[ @(B), @(T), @(K) ]];
  MPSNDArray *inArr = [[MPSNDArray alloc] initWithBuffer:inBuf offset:0 descriptor:inDesc];

  MPSNDArrayDescriptor *wDesc =
      [MPSNDArrayDescriptor descriptorWithDataType:MPSDataTypeFloat32
                                             shape:@[ @(N), @(K) ]];
  wDesc.preferPackedRows = YES;
  [wDesc transposeDimension:0 withDimension:1];  // logical (K, N), like PyTorch
  MPSNDArray *wArr = [[MPSNDArray alloc] initWithBuffer:wBuf offset:0 descriptor:wDesc];

  MPSNDArrayDescriptor *bDesc =
      [MPSNDArrayDescriptor descriptorWithDataType:MPSDataTypeFloat32 shape:@[ @(N) ]];
  MPSNDArray *bArr = [[MPSNDArray alloc] initWithBuffer:bBuf offset:0 descriptor:bDesc];

  MPSNDArrayDescriptor *outDesc =
      [MPSNDArrayDescriptor descriptorWithDataType:MPSDataTypeFloat32
                                             shape:@[ @(B), @(T), @(N) ]];
  MPSNDArray *outArr = [[MPSNDArray alloc] initWithBuffer:outBuf offset:0 descriptor:outDesc];

  MPSNDArrayMatrixMultiplication *kernel =
      [[MPSNDArrayMatrixMultiplication alloc] initWithDevice:dev sourceCount:withBias ? 3 : 2];

  id<MTLCommandBuffer> cmdBuf = [queue commandBuffer];
  id<MTLComputeCommandEncoder> enc = [cmdBuf computeCommandEncoder];
  NSArray *sources = withBias ? @[ inArr, wArr, bArr ] : @[ inArr, wArr ];
  [kernel encodeToCommandEncoder:enc commandBuffer:cmdBuf sourceArrays:sources destinationArray:outArr];
  [enc endEncoding];
  [cmdBuf commit];
  [cmdBuf waitUntilCompleted];

  NSUInteger badRows = 0, firstBad = NSUIntegerMax;
  BOOL row0HasRow65536 = NO;
  for (NSUInteger b = 0; b < B; b++) {
    BOOL bad = NO;
    for (NSUInteger t = 0; t < T && !bad; t++)
      for (NSUInteger n = 0; n < N && !bad; n++) {
        float exp = expectedOut(b, t, n) - (withBias ? 0.0f : biasVal(n));
        if (fabsf(out[(b * T + t) * N + n] - exp) > 1e-2f) bad = YES;
      }
    if (bad) {
      badRows++;
      if (firstBad == NSUIntegerMax) firstBad = b;
    }
  }
  if (B > 65536) {
    row0HasRow65536 = YES;
    for (NSUInteger t = 0; t < T && row0HasRow65536; t++)
      for (NSUInteger n = 0; n < N && row0HasRow65536; n++) {
        float exp = expectedOut(65536, t, n) - (withBias ? 0.0f : biasVal(n));
        if (fabsf(out[t * N + n] - exp) > 1e-2f) row0HasRow65536 = NO;
      }
  }
  printf("B=%7lu bias=%d  bad rows: %6lu  first bad: %ld  row0==row65536's result: %s\n",
         (unsigned long)B, withBias, (unsigned long)badRows,
         firstBad == NSUIntegerMax ? -1L : (long)firstBad,
         B > 65536 ? (row0HasRow65536 ? "YES" : "no") : "n/a");
}

int main(void) {
  @autoreleasepool {
    id<MTLDevice> dev = MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice();
    id<MTLCommandQueue> queue = [dev newCommandQueue];
    printf("device: %s\n", dev.name.UTF8String);
    runCase(dev, queue, 65536, YES);
    runCase(dev, queue, 65537, YES);
    runCase(dev, queue, 80000, YES);
    runCase(dev, queue, 80000, NO);
  }
  return 0;
}

Build & run:
clang -fobjc-arc -framework Foundation -framework Metal \
      -framework MetalPerformanceShaders mps_ndarray_repro.m -o repro && ./repro

Output on Apple M5 Max, macOS 26.5.2:
B=  65536 bias=1  bad rows:      0  first bad: -1  row0==row65536's result: n/a
B=  65537 bias=1  bad rows:     65  first bad: 0  row0==row65536's result: YES
B=  80000 bias=1  bad rows:  28928  first bad: 0  row0==row65536's result: YES
B=  80000 bias=0  bad rows:      0  first bad: -1  row0==row65536's result: no

Versions

PyTorch: 2.12.0, 2.13.0, 2.14.0.dev20260709 (all reproduce; arm64 wheels)
macOS: 26.5.2 (build 25F84)
Hardware: Apple M5 Max
Python: 3.12.13

